description:
The 2026-11-11 19:00 sync run created ~215 GitHub issues that re-filed content already present on GitHub, then imported them back as duplicate beads. Cleaned up on 2026-11-13: 268 duplicate GH issues closed (duplicate label + canonical pointer), 268 duplicate beads closed, 69 stale GH issues closed where the bead was already closed. Post-cleanup the trackers are 1:1 (333 open GH issues = 333 open beads with links; 9 local-only beads).

Remaining risk: 'bd github sync' is bidirectional and creates a new issue for a local bead without an external_ref. Any future run can repeat the burst if a bead's link is missing or mismatched. Before running a real sync again:
- capture a dry run and confirm there are no 'create' intents for beads whose content already exists on GitHub;
- consider matching by normalized title/body before creating, or requiring an explicit push list ('bd github push') rather than sync-created issues;
- the 9 local-only beads (gkll epic + children, qlli, cksc, 4pmm, rv4n, ak18) currently have no external_ref and would be pushed by a sync.

Evidence: /tmp/opencode (cleanup-map.json, gh-close-fails.json, bd-sync-dryrun.log) and the 268-pair body-similarity check (>=0.95).

Users and reviewers cannot see WHY a call was allowed, asked, denied or withheld. kilocode tags every rule with its origin (agent/global/project/yolo/session/manual — kilocode/permission/provenance.ts) and opencode attaches request source metadata (tool + messageID).

Once Authorize() returns {Decision, Reason, RuleOrigin}:
- record the decision as a shepherd_trace fact on the tool call (tool, decision, reason, winning rule index + origin);
- add `yaah permissions explain <tool> [--args <json>]` (doctor-adjacent) that prints the full chain — persona narrowing, level, mode floor, MCP policy, matching rules in order, final decision — without executing;
- include the winning rule origin in approval prompts and denial messages.

Acceptance: trace fact present for every tool call; explain output matches an actual run for the matrix cases; no execution side effects; tests.

Umbrella epic for the permission/approval/mode findings from the 2026-11-10 comparison.

Four problem classes, ordered by risk:
(a) privilege escalation and silent-policy holes (self-editing role/config files; `ask` inert in sub-agents);
(b) duplicated and dead policy machinery (two permission engines, three authorization entry points, three disagreeing tool sets, unwired SubAgentsOnly);
(c) missing approval durability and inconsistent surfaces (ACP remembers by tool name only; TUI/web have no always; no project scope);
(d) read-only ergonomics (shell withheld entirely, so plan mode cannot run rg/git log).

Parity targets: opencode V2's ordered ruleset + save patterns + deny-before-saved + project scope; kilocode's hard rulesets, config-path protection, read-only bash allowlist, headless deny, forceAsk for skill-shell/sandbox escalation.

Deliberately NOT copied from opencode: "read-only is just an agent" with no executable floor (Plan inherits allow * and shell stays permitted), and whollyDisabled's last-rule-per-action hiding heuristic. yaah keeps the explicit capability set + non-approvable GateWithheld.
